  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frontal boundary has made little progress\nsouthward since this evening's update, currently draped across\nsouthern VA while pre-frontal trough remains largely stationary\nfrom Norfolk and Hampton Roads into the Research Triangle\nregion. Trough axis remains very active this morning as robust\nLLJ of SW winds 30-40 kt continues to feed anomalously deep\ntropical moisture off the eastern periphery of TC Bertha over\nthe boundary. Overall kinematics may weaken slightly tonight but\nnot enough to prevent ongoing rounds of showers and a few\nthunderstorms.\n\nThe pre-frontal trough and associated low-level convergence will\nbe slow to shift southward this morning - WoFs runs overnight\npoint to the axis of heaviest rainfall through 12z likely\nsitting north of Kinston and potentially setting up across\nwestern portions of Pitt and Martin counties. If more robust\nconvection does end up training over this corridor flash\nflooding could develop in the next few hours over this area.\n\nLow-level convergence will gradually shift south from VA into \nnorthern NC later this morning as the above-mentioned front and\npre- frontal trough begin to phase. The front is then expected \nto stall across ENC later today, and may not push offshore \nuntil sometime on Saturday. Where the front ends up stalling\nremains uncertain; a more progressive solution yesterday morning\nhas now reverted back to a slower progression in the 00z suite.\n\nA plume of anomalous, and deep, moisture is forecast to reside \nalong the stalled frontal zone, with lift aided by periods of \nWAA and large-scale support from passing shortwaves. Rainfall \nrates will be aided by moderate instability through at least \nFriday, then instability begins to wane after that. The overall \nsynoptic pattern supports periods of heavy rain and excessive \nrainfall rates with an accompanying risk of flooding and flash \nflooding. This is also supported by various forecast aids, \nincluding the ECMWF EFI, which continues to show a strong \nsignal for heavy rain over the area through tonight.\n\nGiven the continued lack of run-to-run consistency on frontal\nplacement and progression, where the strongest low-level forcing\nand thus most intense rainfall rates will occur remains a bit of\na question mark. Most guidance still shows rates shifting south\nrather quickly this afternoon to along the southern coastline,\nbut more members now suggest the front will hang up somewhere\nbetween New Bern and the coast with multiple rounds of\nconvection likely hanging on through Friday. Although this would\nfavor less severe flooding concerns to the north where\noverrunning rainfall would be more likely, this would increase\nthe potential for more extreme rainfall totals of up to 5-8\"\nalong the NC24 corridor and areas north. This signal is\nparticularly strong on Friday, where the REFS has probabilities\nof totals exceeding 5\" of 40-50% for this very area.\n\nOverall, guidance ultimately changed little with rainfall \ntotals with the Flood Watch still reflecting areal average\namounts of 3-5\" with a risk of locally higher amounts. Areas\nalong NC11 and west, extending northeast to the VA border remain\nin a Moderate (Level 3/4) risk of excessive rainfall for today\nwhile the rest of the area sits in Slight (Level 2/4). Slight\nrisks remain for the whole area on Friday and along the southern\ncoastline into Saturday.\n\nA very marginal severe risk remains possible early this morning\ninto tomorrow mainly along and north of Highway 264. Deep, \nmoist profiles should tend to favor water- loaded downdrafts, \nbut some enhanced low- level shear may support a brief/weak \ntornado.",
